From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris Sotraidis <56282582+chrissotraidis@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 18:09:02 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] port: give the [match] line the wall clock and update count
 behind it

The [match] diagnostic carries a game clock value, and a clock value on its own is not a rate: doc 34 asks a run's in-game clock progression and its fixed-update rate to be compared with the desktop reference within 2% over a minute of play, which needs two samples of the same quantity across wall time. ms= is the monotonic instant the line was written and upd= is the count of cGame::Update calls behind it, so a reducer can divide by the interval and get both rates, and a line that was skipped or repeated shows up as a jump in upd rather than as drift in a ratio.

t= carries milliseconds now instead of tenths for the same reason: at 60 lines a second over a sixty-second window, a tenth of a second of quantisation is up to 5% of the window, coarser than the 2% the row allows. The cadence above it is untouched -- upd still advances by exactly 60 between two printed lines.
---
 smstrikers-port/src/Game/Game.cpp | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/smstrikers-port/src/Game/Game.cpp b/smstrikers-port/src/Game/Game.cpp
index 9681376..4c09c68 100644
--- a/smstrikers-port/src/Game/Game.cpp
+++ b/smstrikers-port/src/Game/Game.cpp
@@ -49,6 +49,7 @@ extern cBall* g_pBall;
 extern eCameraType g_eCurrentCameraType;   // Camera/CameraMan.cpp
 extern PowerupBase* g_pPowerups[25];       // AI/Powerups.cpp
 #include "port/benchmark.h"
+#include "port/host.h"     // PORT: port_monotonic_ns, the wall instant the [match] line carries
 extern PowerupBase* g_pPowerups[];
 extern cCharacter* g_pCurrentlyUpdatingCharacter;
 extern cTeam* g_pCurrentlyUpdatingTeam;
@@ -1348,8 +1349,18 @@ void cGame::Update(float deltaTime)
             && g_pBall != NULL && m_pGameClock != NULL)
         {
             const nlVector3& b = g_pBall->GetPosition();
-            OSReport("[match] t=%.1f/%.1f state=%d ot=%d score %d-%d "
+            // PORT: ms and upd are what make this line a rate rather than a state. doc 34 asks a
+            // run's in-game clock progression and its fixed-update rate to be compared with the
+            // desktop reference within 2%, and neither can be read from a clock value on its own:
+            // t is in game seconds, this is the wall instant and the count of updates behind it.
+            // upd advances by exactly 60 between two lines -- the same 60 the cadence below counts
+            // -- so a line that skips or repeats is visible as a jump rather than as drift.
+            // t carries milliseconds rather than tenths because the rate below is a ratio of two
+            // of these samples across a minute: a tenth of a second per line quantises a
+            // sixty-second window by up to 5%, which is coarser than the 2% the row allows.
+            OSReport("[match] ms=%llu upd=%d t=%.3f/%.3f state=%d ot=%d score %d-%d "
                      "ball=(%.2f,%.2f,%.2f)\n",
+                     (unsigned long long)(port_monotonic_ns() / 1000000ull), nMatchLog - 1,
                      (double)m_pGameClock->m_fTimer, (double)m_fGameDuration,
                      (int)GetGameState(), (int)mInSuddenDeath,
                      g_pTeams[0]->m_nScore, g_pTeams[1]->m_nScore,
